## What happened

`DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS because **Apple's security frameworks don't implement DSA**. In earlier .NET versions, the runtime fell back to an OpenSSL shim on macOS for DSA; that fallback has been removed. Windows (CNG) and Linux (OpenSSL) still support DSA natively, so only macOS breaks.

## How to fix it

Since you only need to **verify** legacy signatures, the simplest cross-platform fix is to use **BouncyCastle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle` or `BouncyCastle.Cryptography` on NuGet):

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters publicKey)
{
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

## Other options

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **BouncyCastle** (recommended) | Pure managed code, works everywhere | Extra dependency |
| Ship OpenSSL on macOS + P/Invoke | No new managed deps | Complex packaging/maintenance |
| Runtime check + skip on macOS | Zero changes | Loses macOS coverage |

## Going forward

DSA is considered legacy — plan to migrate new signing to **ECDSA** or **EdDSA**, which are supported on all platforms natively.
